WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.000
CALUM BARNES: Hi, my name's
Calum Barnes from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.693
I'm the Product Manager
for Cloud Speech,

00:00:10.693 --> 00:00:12.110
and today, I'm
going to be talking

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.440
about measuring and improving
speech-to-text accuracy

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.697
with the Google
Cloud speech product.

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:21.030
First, I'm going to give you
a little bit of an overview

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.980
on Cloud Speech-to-Text.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.040
Then I'm going to talk
about how you can measure

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.390
the accuracy of speech
to text on your own data

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:35.400
then what you can do using our
tools to improve that accuracy

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.850
once you've measured it.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.260
And finally, I will do a quick
worked example at the end

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.400
and link you out to some of the
companion tools and examples

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.198
that we've created to go with
this next on-air presentation.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:57.050
So Google Cloud Speech-to-Text
is a API which accepts audio.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.210
It identifies speech
within that audio

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.110
and returns the text
representation of that speech.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:07.190
This happens in real
time or in batch mode.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:12.440
We support both in all
71 languages and 127

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.220
local variants that we support.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.430
We also have, for each
one of these languages,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.250
a massive vocabulary that goes
far beyond the dictionary.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.700
It covers local vernacular,
proper nouns, et cetera.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.370
Today, we're going to be
especially focused on the tools

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.430
that we have for
customizing the API

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.750
and improving recognition
on specific terms

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.090
as well as doing things like
changing the output formatting.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.000
So let's jump in and start
talking about speech accuracy.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.360
Accuracy is pretty much
the most important thing

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.860
when it comes to speech to text.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.350
So whether you're doing
post-processing on the text

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.650
to determine someone's
intent and extract entities

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:53.280
or displaying the text
directly to users for captions,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.030
having a higher quality
transcript that's more accurate

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.170
is going to result in a better
user experience either way.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.000
There are a whole
number of factors

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.390
that can affect speech
accuracy, from background noise

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.630
to audio quality to
various different accents

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.560
or strange verbalizations,
and all of these things

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.450
can have a big impact.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.120
At Google Cloud and
Google as a whole,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.880
we have tried to make our
models as robust as possible,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.500
and this means that they should
work really, really well right

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.170
out of the box on a
wide variety of types

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.760
of speech and other scenarios.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.250
But all speech
recognition systems

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.870
are very sensitive to
input data, and that means,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.910
when you think about
accuracy, you really

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.890
need to be thinking about,
what is the accuracy

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.900
of this system on
my specific data,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.020
in my recorded environment,
the types of speakers I have,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.770
the types of speech it is?

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.070
And to do that, you
have to measure it,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.140
and to measure it
we need to have

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.840
a common language for how we
talk about what the quality

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:00.755
level is.

00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:07.510
And in Speech-to-Text, usually
that's Word Error Rate or WER.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.030
WER or Word Error Rate
is not the only way

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.400
to measure speech
accuracy, but it certainly

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.560
is the most common,
and even if you're also

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.080
looking at other metrics,
which can be valuable,

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.480
usually they're looked at in
conjunction with Word Error

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.100
Rate as well.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.320
Word Error Rate is composed
of three different types

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.960
of errors that can happen
during transcription.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.950
One is the number of insertions.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.600
So these are words which are
present in the hypothesis

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.400
or the machine-generated,
in this case,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.790
transcript that are not
present in the actual ground

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.560
truth accurate transcript.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.700
Substitutions are the number of
words which are present but are

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.160
mistranscribed as
a different word

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.180
or formatted in
an incorrect way,

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.000
and finally, deletions
are the number

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.650
of words which are
completely missing

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.360
from the hypothesis
transcript which

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.325
are supposed to be present
in the source audio or ground

00:03:57.325 --> 00:03:58.680
truth transcript.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.560
You add up all three
of these numbers,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.600
and you divide by the
total number of words

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.850
that exist in the
ground truth reference,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.008
and that gives you the
overall word error rate.

00:04:09.008 --> 00:04:11.550
This means that it is actually
possible for a Word Error Rate

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.825
to be greater than
100% in situations

00:04:13.825 --> 00:04:15.450
where you have very,
very poor quality.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.822
So to measure Word
Error Rate, we

00:04:20.822 --> 00:04:22.780
have a few steps you can
follow, and we've also

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.290
created a bunch of tools to
make it as easy as possible.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:28.710
But it all starts with
getting your audio files.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:32.760
Like I said before, if you
want to measure accuracy

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.010
and how the system is going to
perform in how you're using it,

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.000
you must have
in-domain audio which

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.550
is similar to what
you're going to be doing.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.060
Generally, we recommend having
about three hours in this test

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.970
set, but you can still
get pretty statistically

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.640
significant results with
as little as 30 minutes

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.650
of in-domain audio.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.390
It's more important
that the audio

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.820
is similar and
representative of what you're

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.590
trying to transcribe than
it is to have a ton of it.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.020
The second step is probably
the hardest of all of them.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.185
You have to get the ground
truth for this audio.

00:05:06.185 --> 00:05:11.030
So this means you must have
the human-transcribed 100%

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.960
or as close to 100% as
possible accurate version

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.030
of the transcribed
audio in order

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.150
to compare the various
hypotheses against.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.120
The third step is easy.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.537
You get your machine
transcription

00:05:22.537 --> 00:05:24.920
by sending it to the
Cloud Speech API,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.440
and then finally, you can
compute the Word Error Rate

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.290
with our simple WER
script where you

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.990
feed in the hypothesis
and the ground truth.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.660
And we will show you what
the Word Error Rate is.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.760
That script, in
addition telling you

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.360
the number of insertions,
deletions, and substitutions,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.500
will also give you this pretty
printed HTML that will show you

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.640
exactly where each error is
and what type of error it is,

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.780
and you can see a
sample of that here.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.180
So now that you've measured
the accuracy of your audio,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.460
it's time to think
about what you

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:04.400
can do to improve the
accuracy on your test set

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.820
and on your wider
set, and we do this

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.560
with a set of tools that allow
us to help customize the model

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.020
or customize the system
towards the type of audio

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.600
that we're sending it.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.090
Broadly speaking, there's
three different ways

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.810
to think about improving
speech accuracy through model

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.760
customization.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.700
The first is to customize
the model to your domain

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:31.440
by providing
contextual information.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.200
An example of this
would be if you

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.140
knew that people we're going
to be talking about pizza

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.220
or ordering pizza.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.340
You could bias towards
different types

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.840
of crust, different
types of cheese,

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.820
different toppings, et cetera.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.990
Next is to tweak weights to
address specific word or phrase

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.110
issues.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.030
So commonly this can
occur with proper nouns,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:57.250
people's names, or maybe it's
individual product names,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.240
but words which,
generally speaking,

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.100
occur very rarely in all speech.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:06.220
Number three is to
use context that you

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.800
have to bias towards
specific types of information

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.210
or specific situations.

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:16.810
So for example, if you
had a phone system IVR,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:20.290
you might know that a user is
about to tell you an account

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.000
number or a phone
number, and you

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.820
can tell the ASR
system to only look

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.965
for a digit sequence or
a alphanumeric sequence.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:35.110
We support doing all three
types of these customizations

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.400
in Google Cloud Speech
through the use of our speech

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.890
adaptation tools.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.970
These tools, if used correctly,
can be extremely powerful

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.270
and can shift quality
very significantly

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.390
depending on the situation and
the type of bias in what you're

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:51.900
doing.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.780
Before we get into the
features of speech adaptation

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.210
and exactly how to
use them, though, I

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.240
want to talk a little bit about
how speech adaptation works

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.550
and how that's somewhat
different from other types

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.350
of model customization.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.860
In order to talk about
how we customize the model

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.420
and customize the
results, we need

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.800
to understand a little bit
more about how ASR or Automatic

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.900
Speech Recognition systems work.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.930
Now, this is a extremely
simplified diagram,

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.450
and not all speech
recognition systems

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.130
work in this exact same way.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.690
But it will help us
illustrate the point here.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.820
So a very simple system would
take audio input in from a user

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.680
into what's called
an acoustic model.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.350
The acoustic model
looks at the audio wave

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.700
forms or the spectrogram, and
it converts these wave forms

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.140
into sounds which it thinks are
there, also known as phonemes.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.070
The language model then looks
at the groupings and series

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.100
of phonemes and
tries to understand

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.190
from those sounds which words
or phrases are being said.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:58.290
Most language models also
take context and other factors

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.890
into account when
determining the text which

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.630
the phonemes represent.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.240
Some models will
produce a single output,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.750
or perhaps it will have
n best alternatives that

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:13.920
could be determined from
that grouping of phonemes.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.570
Now let's take a look
at the simplified

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.210
version of the Google
Speech-to-Text API.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.310
The audio comes into
an acoustic model

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.992
and is converted
to phonemes that

00:09:24.992 --> 00:09:26.450
are set to the
language model, just

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:28.610
like we talked about before.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.110
The difference is two things.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.520
One is the language model
produces, instead of

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.830
just a single highest confidence
hypothesis or a series

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:43.720
of n best alternatives-- but
it produces an entire lattice

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:46.400
of potential word alternatives.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:49.460
The other difference is
the speech adaptation API,

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:53.230
which allows users to
send context and word

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:57.160
hints about what the
audio might contain.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.820
This information is then used
to operate on the word lattice

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.250
and determine, based
on these hints as well

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:06.130
as the confidences from
the language model, what

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:10.060
the highest confidence
hypothesis text is as well

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:14.140
as n best alternatives if that's
interesting to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.770
This differs slightly from other
types of model customization

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:20.500
where you might have a
custom language model where

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:23.320
the entire language
model itself is changed

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.440
to account for
specific proper nouns

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:30.140
or various different sequences
which might be expected.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.890
But the great part
about speech adaptation

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:34.690
is there's no training
or retraining required,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:38.340
so it's much easier to
experiment and much cheaper

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:43.720
to adapt the model to a
variety of different needs.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.560
Next is it happens
completely in real time,

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:48.460
so we're able to
compute these changes

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:53.850
without adding any latency to
the end-to-end ASR pipeline.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.990
And this also means,
because there is no training

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.050
and it happens in real time,
you can have a different context

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.950
with every single
request that you send.

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:04.600
So one request can bias
towards a digit sequence,

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:08.320
and the next request can
bias towards somebody's name,

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:09.100
for example.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.220
All of this can be accomplished
with the speech adaptation API

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:13.980
that I was talking about before.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:17.560
The speech adaptation API today
has three different components

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:18.460
or options.

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:20.230
First is phrase hints.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:23.620
This is the ability to send
words or long phrases which

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:27.040
you think may be present in
the speech or in the text,

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.380
and the system will
do its best to look

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.440
at the words and
combinations of words

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:35.380
biased towards that particular
piece of information.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:38.380
In some cases, though,
that might not be enough

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.560
or might not be giving you
granular enough control,

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:44.260
and that's what the
boost API is for.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:49.970
The boost API allows you to
specify an actual weight value

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:53.030
for a specific
word or bigram that

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:58.550
will then take effect on that
word or bigram in the word

00:11:58.550 --> 00:11:59.120
lattice.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.070
This is especially
useful for things

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:05.660
like proper nouns or rare words
because you can significantly

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.360
boost the likelihood of
them being recommended.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.680
Finally, we have
the classes feature,

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.100
which is essentially pre-built
phrase hints and boost

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.290
values for common scenarios.

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.610
For example, an alphanumeric
sequence or a digit

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:25.220
sequence, being able to
recognize that without having

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:27.680
to set the values yourself.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.900
Coming soon, we will
have some additions

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:33.590
to the speech
adaptation API, namely

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.760
custom classes which will
allow users to create and share

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:42.020
their own premade
classes that can then

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.900
be used with boost or speech
adaptation the same way

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:46.760
classes can today.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.420
This doesn't make the system
necessarily any more accurate,

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:53.150
but it gives users much
greater composability

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:57.560
when they are working on
these problems in the way

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:01.010
that they can specify
phrases or words that they

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:03.650
want to bias towards.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.930
Next is saved
context, which allows

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:11.330
you to save one or many known
good biasing configurations

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:15.530
and specify that entire phrase
list or boost list with just

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:18.140
an ID on subsequent calls.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:21.110
This can be very useful in
saving bandwidth overhead

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.930
for users that need to send
thousands or tens of thousands

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:25.970
of words in each context.

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:34.220
So that gives you an
overview of the biasing tools

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:35.810
and the model
customization tools

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:40.820
that we have available
within the Google speech API.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.890
We're always trying
to improve these,

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:48.170
but ultimately, these
tools are just how you send

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:51.110
and how you give the
biasing information.

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.210
The real hard part
is figuring out

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.285
what the right biasing
information to send to the API

00:13:56.285 --> 00:14:01.630
is, and that's what we're
going to talk about next.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.500
So broadly, some
things to consider

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:09.150
are, what am I doing
with this transcript?

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:12.210
So when you are
transcribing the audio,

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:16.230
is the result going
into some NLU system

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:18.270
where you need to
extract an entity?

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.850
Is it being displayed
directly to a user?

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:24.930
Are there specific things
that the downstream systems

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:26.490
are going to be sensitive to?

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:28.740
If your goal is to
capture a phone number,

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.415
then you have to be
absolutely positive

00:14:30.415 --> 00:14:32.040
that you're getting
that digit sequence

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.060
right every single time.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:36.890
Next thing to
consider is, are there

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.320
rare words or proper nouns?

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:44.000
Rare words and proper nouns are
very difficult for ASR systems

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:49.010
because, statistically
speaking, they occur very, very

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.730
uncommonly in everyday
speech, so it's less likely

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:57.530
that a speech-to-text system
is going to recommend that that

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.740
is the word in the series
of phonemes as opposed

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:04.500
to a homonym of a
more common word.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.070
This can be
complicated, but this

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:11.240
is why biasing towards these
words very, very highly

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:15.860
can cause them to be recommended
at the rate which you want.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:20.150
The next is, what contextual
information can I use?

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.210
So this is, what external
information outside

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:26.510
of the audio itself
can I use to figure out

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.100
what the person might
be talking about?

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:33.320
So examples of this would be
context or state in a chat bot

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:34.280
application.

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:35.810
Maybe you have a user history.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:39.950
So you know the types of queries
that the user usually makes,

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:41.510
and you can use that
to help increase

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.605
accuracy on future queries.

00:15:44.605 --> 00:15:46.730
And that feeds into the
final point, which is, do I

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:48.445
have strong or weak context?

00:15:48.445 --> 00:15:50.570
So when you think about
that contextual information

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:53.460
that you have, you
should be thinking about,

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:56.420
do I know exactly what
the user is going to say

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.300
and I'm pretty sure I know how
they're going to verbalize it?

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.190
Or do I just know
broad categories

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:03.770
of what this is about?

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:07.100
Some examples of strong
context would be a IVR,

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:11.540
like a phone-answering bot
scenario where exactly that

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.900
the user is about to give a
phone number, or say yes or no,

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.040
or something like that.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:20.090
Next would be a system for
giving commands or [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:22.560
system where that
the user is going

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:28.840
to say, change the channel,
or play songs by some artist.

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:32.200
In these systems, you have very,
very constrained vocabulary,

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.920
and this can help you to
bias and increase accuracy

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.640
on that specific vocabulary.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.040
And finally, as I talked
about before, important words

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.248
or entities--

00:16:42.248 --> 00:16:44.790
if you have a proper noun that's
very important to transcribe

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:46.770
correctly, you're
almost always going

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.730
to need to bias towards
it very, very strongly.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.450
Next, we have weak
context, which

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:59.610
is a situation like captions,
or dictation, or perhaps

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.290
a conversation
between two people

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:03.810
or multiple people in a meeting.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:05.252
And these are
situations where you

00:17:05.252 --> 00:17:06.960
don't know exactly
what somebody is going

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.200
to say at any one moment,
but you know broadly what

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.770
they are talking about.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.890
For example, this
recording is all

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.319
about speech-to-text
technology, and that

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:19.740
might give you hints about
what words are going to be said

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.800
or are common that you can
use to increase accuracy

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:25.060
on those words.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.180
So now that we've looked
at the biasing tools

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.070
that are available
as well as what

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.330
to think about when you're
starting to think about biasing

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.380
and model
customization, I'm going

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:38.940
to take you through a demo or
really more of a worked example

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:43.650
on what it looks like
to do biasing for real.

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.180
Now, this is going to be
a very simple example.

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:49.200
We've also created
a bunch of content

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.420
to go along with this
next on-air session

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.270
where you'll actually be able
to try all of this for yourself

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:54.900
in a quick lap.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:00.560
For the purpose
of this demo, I'm

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.050
going to be focusing
on improvement type two

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.300
that I talked about where
we're going to tweak weights

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.720
to address specific words and
phrases issues, especially

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:11.510
focused on rare words and
proper nouns, as you'll see.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.090
So to get started
here, I'm going

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:19.160
to do basically exactly what
I told you not to do before,

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:22.600
which is I'm going to give
this example based on just one

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:24.070
phrase and one sentence.

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:26.560
I don't have a whole
corpus of audio here,

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:31.510
but the goal is not necessarily
to vastly improve this one

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.370
phrase, but to
just show you how I

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.530
think about the biasing
problem and the signals

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.320
that you can use and
apply to a larger corpus

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.330
when you're doing this
yourself or trying it out

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:41.900
in the quick lap.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:45.102
So I've recorded a single
sentence of me speaking here

00:18:45.102 --> 00:18:46.060
that I'll play for you.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:46.727
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:18:46.727 --> 00:18:48.020
- Hi, this is Calum.

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:49.657
I'm talking about
the speakotron.

00:18:49.657 --> 00:18:50.240
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:54.080
So you can see I say my
name, a proper noun, as well

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.870
as a totally made-up
word, speakotron.

00:18:56.870 --> 00:19:00.210
Now, even with these
rare words, like I said,

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.030
the Google recognizer works
very well out of the box,

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:07.180
so I actually didn't have any
issues recognizing this audio

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:08.030
originally.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:10.220
So I had to make the
problem a little bit harder

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.000
for our speech
recognition system,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.730
and to do that, I
added some noise

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.350
to this same audio recording.

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:16.850
And this is the
audio recording I'll

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:20.250
be using for all the future
examples in the demo,

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:21.640
so I'll play that for you now.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:22.307
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:19:22.307 --> 00:19:23.180
Hi, this is Calum.

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:26.017
I'm talking about
the speakotron.

00:19:26.017 --> 00:19:26.600
[END PLAYBACK]

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.600
So as you can see, exact same
sentence with some white noise

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.850
added in the
background that made it

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:34.640
so that the ASR system
wasn't recognizing it

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:35.870
right out of the box.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:38.660
On the right-hand side,
I've set up some Python code

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:42.470
to try out sending this to
the Cloud speech recognizer.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.850
I've specified my input file.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:48.590
I'm using US English since I'm
a American English speaker.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:51.830
This file is a
basic linear PCM WAV

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:56.060
file recorded at 16 kilohertz.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:57.782
I'm not sending
any speech context

00:19:57.782 --> 00:19:59.240
yet because I just
want to see what

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.530
the bare usage of the system is.

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:05.150
Next, I need to setup
my simple WER script,

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:09.080
so I'm putting in my ground
truth hypothesis here,

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:09.910
hi, this is Calum.

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:11.450
I'm talking about
the speakotron.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:14.150
I haven't included any
punctuation in this case,

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:15.740
but you could
include punctuation

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.350
if you're expecting punctuation
in the result as well.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.620
The hypothesis will come
directly from the Google speech

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.320
transcript that I just
set up, and then we

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.990
will compute the error rate
and pre-print the HTML.

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.890
So let's take a look at what the
accuracy looks like right out

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.110
of the box on this noisy file.

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:37.550
I had a word error
rate of 37.5%, driven

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.800
all by substitution error here.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.050
So we can see it
got my name wrong.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:45.740
Not that wrong, but it
is spelled incorrectly.

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:49.100
But more concerning,
instead of "the speakotron",

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:53.450
it was transcribed as "this
Eco tron", which is not good.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:56.570
If we were really worried about
the speakotron product here,

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.430
then we would have
not even captured

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.670
that this phrase was about
the speakotron product.

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:03.590
So let's look at what we can do.

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:07.100
Using the phrase hint API, I
could do something very simple

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.310
like put the exact right
transcription in as a phrase,

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:11.075
hi, this is Calum.

00:21:11.075 --> 00:21:13.450
I'm talking about
the speakotron.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.160
Now, this did work.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.680
This did result in
0% Word Error Rate

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:21.260
and the phrase being
transcribed perfectly.

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:22.900
This is always a
good thing to try out

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:25.450
if you have a single phrase.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:28.480
It basically just shows you
that speech adaptation as tools

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:29.200
work.

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:32.860
We are actually operating on
the lattice and changing things,

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:34.420
but this isn't
that helpful to you

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:37.000
as you think about
improving recognition

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.180
across an entire corpus
because if you knew what

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.520
the correct transcription
of the phrase was,

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:45.497
you wouldn't be sending it to
the speech API to begin with.

00:21:45.497 --> 00:21:47.080
So I don't think
this is that helpful,

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:49.090
and sending the whole
phrase is also not

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:52.100
going to be helpful to other
words where somebody is saying,

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:55.780
hey, this is some
other name, or they

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.582
are verbalizing the phrase
a little bit differently.

00:21:58.582 --> 00:22:01.040
So let's think about what we
can do to increase recognition

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:06.020
without just going for the
full phrase being accurate.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:08.960
We could use the boost API
and look at the rare words,

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:10.910
like I talked
about doing before.

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:15.170
Calum and speakotron here
are the two rare words,

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:18.270
and by boosting them
fairly significantly,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.600
I think we can get pretty
good results on those words.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:25.760
So let's take a look at how that
performed on our noisy version

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:27.990
of the audio.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:31.180
I was able to decrease the Word
Error Rate substantially just

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:34.810
by doing this, where I've got
to spell my name correctly,

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:38.260
and instead of Eco
tron, it has correctly

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.060
recognized speakotron.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:42.010
But there is still an
error of it actually

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:44.210
thinks it's saying
"this" speakotron and not

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:47.340
"the" speakotron.

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:50.700
Now, this is an easy fix
on the phrase side, too,

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.130
but it got me
thinking more broadly

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:55.860
about what you can
do to increase this,

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.110
not just for this one
phrase, but all phrases

00:22:58.110 --> 00:22:59.820
that are talking
about the speakotron

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:02.700
or that are talking about some--
somebody saying their name

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:04.440
is Calum.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.690
And one thing we
can do with boost

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.630
is to boost not just
the unigrams that

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:12.090
are important to us,
but also the bigrams.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:15.150
We wouldn't want to boost really
anything more than a bigram

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:18.360
because you're just going
to significantly confuse

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.690
the lattice since those phrases
are never going to exist

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:23.860
like that in the lattice.

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:27.978
But by thinking about what
are the bigrams where people

00:23:27.978 --> 00:23:29.770
are going to actually
use these rare words,

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:31.700
we can greatly
increase recognition.

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.860
So when somebody's
saying their name,

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:37.840
they're usually going to say
"is" name, my name is Calum,

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:39.930
or this is Calum.

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:43.320
And you could also think
of other verbalizations.

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:45.780
And when I thought about
speakotron, I thought,

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:48.590
people are probably going to
say "the" speakotron or "a"

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:52.410
speakotron, the article
with it basically,

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:56.070
and we can use that to
boost those bi grams as well

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:57.750
as the unigrams
even more strongly.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:01.260
And that's going to help us get
even better recognition in some

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:02.720
of those scenarios.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:06.300
And so in this case, I boosted
the bigram and the unigram.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:09.450
I've chosen boost
values of five and 10.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:12.600
I think you'll have to play with
exactly what boost value works

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:14.753
best for you, but
generally speaking,

00:24:14.753 --> 00:24:16.170
the bigram with
the article should

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:18.930
be boosted at a lower
rate than the unit gram

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:22.590
because you wouldn't want
every single word after "the"

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.900
to be transcribed
as "the" speakotron.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:27.120
And with that, I was
able to actually get down

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:31.800
to 0% Word Error Rate, and we
have an accurate transcript.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:34.260
Like I said, this is just
working on one single phrase,

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:36.360
and you would never
get to 0% Word Error

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.420
Rate on an entire corpus or
multiple different variations

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.540
on this same phrase,
but hopefully this

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.037
gives you a helpful
idea about the way

00:24:45.037 --> 00:24:46.620
to think about these
problems and what

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:49.830
you can do to, overall,
decrease Word Error Rate

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:51.770
across the corpus.

00:24:51.770 --> 00:24:56.150
So if you are interested in
trying this out for yourself

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.790
or finding out more
information about improving

00:24:58.790 --> 00:25:02.330
speech accuracy for your data,
you can check out our docs.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:05.210
We've recently updated the
speech adaptation documentation

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:07.250
to include a number
of the best practices

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.090
that I've just
talked about here.

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.120
If you want to actually
try it out for real,

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.998
we've created a quick lab
specifically to go along

00:25:13.998 --> 00:25:16.040
with this content called
"Measuring and Improving

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:22.280
Speech Accuracy" where you can
try out using the simple word

00:25:22.280 --> 00:25:26.480
script and tuning the
biasing with boost,

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:29.120
and phrase hints,
and classes and try

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:33.610
to achieve the greatest
word error rate reduction

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:34.530
for yourself.

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:36.330
If you're interested
in the features

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:38.520
that I talked about
before the custom

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:42.790
classes or the saved context,
you can apply for alpha access.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:47.940
Calumbarnes@google.com is
how to get in touch with me.

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:50.610
Thanks very much,
and I wish you best

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:54.560
of luck with all of
your speech accuracy.

